{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We showed that compound sparsity severity---the super-additive interaction between head pruning and KV cache eviction---is not phase-invariant but exhibits an emergent phase-strategy interaction spanning over an order of magnitude within a single generation. Under importance-based eviction, the severity gradient grows from $69{\times}$ to $274{\times}$ across sparsity levels ($d_z$: $0.44 \to 0.80$); random eviction shows no such modulation (7/8 conditions non-significant). A crossover at $s{=}0.25$---where importance-based eviction reverses ranking across phases---demonstrates that no single strategy dominates uniformly. The pattern replicates on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct at $s \geq 0.20$ ($d_z \geq 0.55$) and partially extends cross-architecture to DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM) with a qualitatively consistent but substantially weaker trend at $\sim$1/40 the magnitude. Discrimination collapse provides a mechanistic explanation supported by both Qwen2-based models; LLaMA results are directionally consistent but the interaction is weaker than in Qwen2 architectures.
These findings motivate phase-aware sparsity allocation for reasoning model deployment.
